# Trivy CVE allowlist for the shipped TheoDB image (ghcr.io/usetheodev/theo-db).
#
# WHY THIS FILE MUST EXIST AT ALL:
# The reusable publish workflow (usetheodev/theo/.github/workflows/build-publish.yml) passes
# `trivyignores: .trivyignore` to aquasecurity/trivy-action. When the file is ABSENT, Trivy does not
# fall back to "no ignores" — it aborts before scanning:
#
#     ERROR: cannot find ignorefile '.trivyignore'.
#     ##[error]Process completed with exit code 1.
#
# The job then fails in ~2 seconds, which looks like a CVE gate doing its job but is the exact
# opposite: nothing was ever scanned. Every `publish` run died there, so the image was never pushed —
# the root cause of #187 (`docker pull ghcr.io/usetheodev/theo-db:latest` -> `manifest unknown`, the
# first command in the README). An EMPTY-but-present file is what makes the gate actually run.
#
# Format (mirrors .claude/rules/deps-audit-allowlist.txt):
#   CVE-ID   # sunset: YYYY-MM-DD — reason
#
# Sunset discipline per .claude/rules/deps-audit-golden-rule.md § 4:
#   - CRITICAL CVEs: sunset <= 30 days
#   - HIGH CVEs:     sunset <= 60 days
#   - MEDIUM CVEs:   sunset <= 90 days
# An expired entry is NOT honored — the finding re-fires at full severity.
#
# Lines beginning with `#` are comments. Each non-comment line is a bare CVE ID.
#
# INTENTIONALLY EMPTY: no CVE is allowlisted. Adding an entry requires a CHANGELOG note under
# `[Unreleased] § Changed` and — for HIGH/CRITICAL — an ADR, per the deps-audit golden rule.
# If the gate now fails with real CRITICAL/HIGH findings, that is a genuine security result on an
# image that has never been scanned; triage the CVEs, do not pad this file.
